# Connecting "checkout-api"

Route: kind=otlp segment=acme-a1b2c3d4

## Most SDKs need no code change

Every official OpenTelemetry SDK auto-configures its OTLP exporter from
environment variables. Setting the three in the rendered `.env` file
(`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT`, `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL`,
`OTEL_SERVICE_NAME`) before your process starts is enough for traces,
metrics, and logs to start flowing, using whatever auto-instrumentation
your language's SDK ships (`opentelemetry-instrument` for Python, the
`@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node` package for Node.js, a Java
agent jar, and so on) or your own manual SDK initialization — both read
the same env vars.

## Always set OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL explicitly

Several SDKs (the Go, Java, and Python SDKs among them) default to
OTLP/gRPC when this variable is unset. This gateway's tenant routing is
path-prefix based (docs/gateway-tier-plan.md D9) and gRPC's request path
is fixed by the service definition, not configurable — so a client left on
its gRPC default will never reach a route this package's endpoint
describes; it dials the right host but sends a request path no HTTPRoute
matches. The rendered `OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL` value is required, not
optional, for exactly this reason.

## Base endpoint vs. per-signal endpoint

`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT` is a BASE endpoint:

    https://telemetry.example.com/otlp/acme-a1b2c3d4

The OTLP/HTTP exporter appends `v1/traces`, `v1/metrics`, `v1/logs` to it
itself. If your setup instead needs a signal-specific override —
`OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT` and friends — those variables are
used exactly as given, with nothing appended, so it must already include
the signal path, e.g. for traces:

    OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TRACES_ENDPOINT=https://telemetry.example.com/otlp/acme-a1b2c3d4/v1/traces

Only set a per-signal override if you have a reason to (routing one
signal differently); otherwise the base endpoint alone already covers all
three.

## The route segment is an identifier, not a secret

docs/gateway-tier-plan.md §3: the segment in this URL identifies your
tenant to the gateway; it is not an authorization token. The gateway's
real controls are origin allowlists, rate limits, and rotation — treat
the endpoint like any other config value, and rotate it (ask your
platform team) if you believe it has leaked somewhere sensitive.

## Browser RUM (Grafana Faro)

Shepherd does not yet run a Faro receiver (docs/gateway-tier-plan.md D10
— it is demand-driven, not built). If you want browser RUM (error
capture, Web Vitals, session tracking) today, D10 documents a hybrid that
works without it: use the Faro Web SDK for the RUM batteries, but
configure ITS trace exporter (`@grafana/faro-web-tracing`, which already
speaks OTLP) to point at the OTLP endpoint above instead of a Faro
collector endpoint. Traces ride this scalable, gateway-tenanted path; ask
your platform team when Faro support ships if you need the RUM payload
(errors/logs/vitals) ingested too.
